1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,533
Who Really Gets Paid When
You Tap Your Card?

2
00:00:03,693 --> 00:00:05,533
You tap your card.

3
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:07,853
Two seconds later, it beeps.

4
00:00:08,173 --> 00:00:09,173
Done.

5
00:00:09,485 --> 00:00:13,005
But in those two seconds, your money
started a trip through at least three

6
00:00:13,005 --> 00:00:14,045
companies.

7
00:00:14,045 --> 00:00:16,313
And every one of them takes a cut.

8
00:00:16,633 --> 00:00:20,633
Together, those cuts cost American stores
more than one hundred and ninety-eight

9
00:00:20,633 --> 00:00:22,073
billion dollars a year.

10
00:00:22,073 --> 00:00:24,073
Some of it pays for your rewards points.

11
00:00:24,073 --> 00:00:25,073
And some of it...

12
00:00:24,793 --> 00:00:27,942
is paid by people who never
used a card at all.

13
00:00:28,262 --> 00:00:30,742
So we're going to follow
one single purchase.

14
00:00:30,742 --> 00:00:32,822
One hundred dollars, cent by cent.

15
00:00:32,822 --> 00:00:34,662
This is Billions and Bulls.

16
00:00:34,662 --> 00:00:36,582
Let's follow the money.

17
00:00:37,222 --> 00:00:39,620
Part 1 The Two-Second Trip

18
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:43,300
Say you spend one hundred dollars at a
store, and you pay with a rewards

19
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:44,300
credit card.

20
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:45,860
You don't even swipe anymore.

21
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:51,700
In September 2025, Visa says, four out of
five of its in-person payments

22
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:54,180
around the world were made with a tap.

23
00:00:54,926 --> 00:00:58,926
The moment you tap, the card reader sends
a message to the store's bank,

24
00:00:58,926 --> 00:01:01,406
or to the payment company the store uses.

25
00:01:01,406 --> 00:01:04,313
People in the industry call
it the acquirer.

26
00:01:04,473 --> 00:01:07,753
The acquirer passes the message
to the card network.

27
00:01:07,753 --> 00:01:09,513
In this case, Visa.

28
00:01:09,513 --> 00:01:12,953
And Visa sends it to the bank
that gave you the card.

29
00:01:12,953 --> 00:01:14,311
The issuer.

30
00:01:14,711 --> 00:01:16,871
Your bank checks two things.

31
00:01:16,871 --> 00:01:18,311
Is this really you?

32
00:01:18,311 --> 00:01:21,031
And do you have the money, or
the credit, to pay?

33
00:01:21,031 --> 00:01:24,871
If the answer is yes, the approval
races all the way back.

34
00:01:24,871 --> 00:01:25,871
Beep.

35
00:01:26,762 --> 00:01:29,162
Now multiply that by the whole planet.

36
00:01:29,162 --> 00:01:34,522
In its 2025 fiscal year, Visa says its
own network processed two hundred

37
00:01:34,522 --> 00:01:36,559
and fifty-eight billion transactions.

38
00:01:36,719 --> 00:01:40,319
That's roughly eight thousand every
single second, day and night.

39
00:01:41,599 --> 00:01:44,159
But the beep is only a promise.

40
00:01:44,159 --> 00:01:45,731
No money has moved yet.

41
00:01:45,970 --> 00:01:50,530
That happens later, usually overnight, in
two steps the industry calls clearing

42
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:51,530
and settlement.

43
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:54,417
The store's bank sends in
the day's purchases.

44
00:01:54,577 --> 00:01:56,417
The banks settle up through Visa.

45
00:01:56,417 --> 00:02:00,177
And only then does money land
in the store's account.

46
00:02:00,177 --> 00:02:01,177
And here's the thing.

47
00:02:01,137 --> 00:02:03,137
It isn't your one hundred dollars.

48
00:02:03,137 --> 00:02:05,967
Your bank sends the money, minus a fee.

49
00:02:06,127 --> 00:02:08,767
Visa charges its own fees along the way.

50
00:02:08,767 --> 00:02:11,583
And the store's payment
company takes a cut too.

51
00:02:11,743 --> 00:02:16,143
By the time it arrives, the store keeps
somewhere around ninety-seven dollars.

52
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:17,903
With some cards, a little more.

53
00:02:17,903 --> 00:02:19,819
With others, a little less.

54
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:22,379
Three dollars doesn't sound like much.

55
00:02:22,379 --> 00:02:23,419
But it adds up.

56
00:02:23,419 --> 00:02:28,059
In 2025, American merchants paid a record of
more than one hundred and ninety-eight

57
00:02:28,779 --> 00:02:33,339
billion dollars in card processing fees,
according to the Nilson Report.

58
00:02:33,339 --> 00:02:36,059
That's more than half a billion dollars.

59
00:02:36,059 --> 00:02:38,379
Every single day.

60
00:02:38,379 --> 00:02:40,859
So who ends up with all that money?

61
00:02:40,859 --> 00:02:43,463
Let's start with the name on the card.

62
00:02:44,995 --> 00:02:47,315
Part 2 The Toll Road

63
00:02:47,315 --> 00:02:50,835
The story starts in 1958, in
Fresno, California.

64
00:02:50,835 --> 00:02:54,755
Bank of America mailed about sixty
thousand credit cards to people who had

65
00:02:54,755 --> 00:02:55,792
never asked for one.

66
00:02:56,032 --> 00:02:57,152
No application.

67
00:02:57,152 --> 00:03:00,352
The card just showed up in the
mailbox, ready to use.

68
00:03:00,352 --> 00:03:02,352
It was called the BankAmericard.

69
00:03:02,352 --> 00:03:06,628
And stores that took it reportedly paid
a fee of around six percent.

70
00:03:06,948 --> 00:03:08,788
The launch was messy.

71
00:03:08,788 --> 00:03:11,108
Fraud and unpaid bills piled up.

72
00:03:11,108 --> 00:03:12,628
But the idea spread.

73
00:03:12,628 --> 00:03:17,028
Other banks joined in, and in 1976
the system got a new name.

74
00:03:17,028 --> 00:03:18,028
Visa.

75
00:03:17,735 --> 00:03:21,015
For decades, Visa was owned by
the banks that used it.

76
00:03:21,015 --> 00:03:23,487
Then, in 2008, it went public.

77
00:03:23,487 --> 00:03:27,647
The IPO reportedly raised about seventeen
point nine billion dollars, the

78
00:03:27,647 --> 00:03:30,367
biggest in American history at the time.

79
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:32,693
And here's what most people
get wrong about Visa.

80
00:03:32,853 --> 00:03:34,533
Visa doesn't give you a credit card.

81
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:35,813
It doesn't lend you money.

82
00:03:35,813 --> 00:03:37,493
It doesn't set your interest rate.

83
00:03:37,493 --> 00:03:40,213
And it doesn't lose a cent if
you never pay your bill.

84
00:03:40,213 --> 00:03:43,370
Visa says so itself, in its annual report.

85
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:45,450
It is not a financial institution.

86
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:48,970
It does not issue cards, and it
does not extend credit.

87
00:03:49,690 --> 00:03:50,730
So what is it?

88
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:53,299
Think of it as a toll road for money.

89
00:03:53,619 --> 00:03:55,299
Banks own the cars.

90
00:03:55,299 --> 00:03:57,219
Visa owns the road.

91
00:03:57,219 --> 00:04:01,539
And every time money drives across
it, Visa charges a small toll.

92
00:04:01,619 --> 00:04:03,059
The tolls are tiny.

93
00:04:03,059 --> 00:04:04,685
But the traffic is enormous.

94
00:04:04,685 --> 00:04:09,565
In the twelve months to September 2025,
about fourteen point two trillion

95
00:04:09,565 --> 00:04:13,965
dollars in payments moved on Visa's
network, on nearly five billion Visa

96
00:04:13,965 --> 00:04:16,026
cards and other payment credentials.

97
00:04:16,186 --> 00:04:19,706
Visa charges banks for the volume
they push across the road.

98
00:04:19,706 --> 00:04:22,346
It charges for every message it processes.

99
00:04:22,346 --> 00:04:26,026
And some of its richest tolls come from
cross-border payments, when you

100
00:04:26,026 --> 00:04:27,336
pay in another currency.

101
00:04:27,336 --> 00:04:31,656
That line alone brought in more than
fourteen billion dollars.

102
00:04:31,816 --> 00:04:32,978
But there's a twist.

103
00:04:33,138 --> 00:04:35,138
Visa also pays the banks.

104
00:04:35,138 --> 00:04:39,778
Almost sixteen billion dollars last year,
in what it calls client incentives.

105
00:04:39,778 --> 00:04:43,138
Money to put Visa's name on more cards,
and to keep the traffic coming.

106
00:04:44,695 --> 00:04:48,775
Add it all up, and Visa brought in forty
billion dollars in net revenue

107
00:04:48,775 --> 00:04:50,855
in its 2025 fiscal year.

108
00:04:50,855 --> 00:04:53,124
Its net income was about twenty billion.

109
00:04:53,284 --> 00:04:56,644
Visa kept roughly half of
everything it made as profit.

110
00:04:56,644 --> 00:04:59,844
Spread across all those payments, that's
roughly twenty-eight cents for

111
00:04:59,844 --> 00:05:01,834
every hundred dollars on its network.

112
00:05:02,153 --> 00:05:06,794
So if the store loses about three
dollars, and Visa takes only a fraction

113
00:05:06,794 --> 00:05:07,794
of that...

114
00:05:07,673 --> 00:05:08,953
where does the rest go?

115
00:05:08,953 --> 00:05:10,840
To the biggest player in this story.

116
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:14,435
And it's not the one whose
name is on your card.

117
00:05:14,995 --> 00:05:18,115
Part 3 The Fee Visa Sets, but Doesn't Keep

118
00:05:18,115 --> 00:05:19,875
It's called the interchange fee.

119
00:05:19,875 --> 00:05:23,489
When you pay with a card, the store's
bank pays a fee to your bank.

120
00:05:23,649 --> 00:05:27,809
Visa sets the default rates, and publishes
them in a public table that runs

121
00:05:27,809 --> 00:05:29,249
for dozens of pages.

122
00:05:29,249 --> 00:05:31,009
But Visa doesn't keep that fee.

123
00:05:31,009 --> 00:05:33,009
Your bank does.

124
00:05:33,009 --> 00:05:34,602
Why does it exist at all?

125
00:05:34,602 --> 00:05:37,482
Visa's argument is that it
balances the system.

126
00:05:37,482 --> 00:05:39,642
Your bank takes the risk
that you won't pay.

127
00:05:39,642 --> 00:05:40,682
It fights fraud.

128
00:05:40,682 --> 00:05:42,028
It gives you time to pay.

129
00:05:42,028 --> 00:05:45,548
And the store gets a customer who can
spend money they don't have in their

130
00:05:45,548 --> 00:05:46,748
pocket.

131
00:05:46,908 --> 00:05:49,393
But the rate depends on
the card you carry.

132
00:05:49,393 --> 00:05:53,393
According to Visa's own rate table, for a
purchase at a regular store, a

133
00:05:53,393 --> 00:05:56,513
basic credit card carries a fee of
one point four three percent,

134
00:05:57,153 --> 00:05:58,538
plus ten cents.

135
00:05:58,698 --> 00:06:01,658
On our hundred dollars, that's
a dollar fifty-three.

136
00:06:01,658 --> 00:06:05,818
A premium card like Visa Infinite can
carry two point three percent, plus

137
00:06:05,818 --> 00:06:07,018
ten cents.

138
00:06:07,018 --> 00:06:08,858
Two dollars and forty cents.

139
00:06:08,858 --> 00:06:09,858
To your bank.

140
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:13,820
Buy the same thing online, and the premium
rate can go up to two point six

141
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:15,660
percent, plus ten cents.

142
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:18,060
Two dollars and seventy cents.

143
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:21,225
Now pay with a debit card from
a big bank instead.

144
00:06:21,385 --> 00:06:26,025
In the United States, that fee is capped
by law at twenty-one cents, plus

145
00:06:26,025 --> 00:06:29,726
five hundredths of a percent, plus
one cent for fraud prevention.

146
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:33,806
On the same hundred dollars, that's
about twenty-seven cents.

147
00:06:34,046 --> 00:06:35,166
Same store.

148
00:06:35,166 --> 00:06:36,766
Same hundred dollars.

149
00:06:36,766 --> 00:06:40,366
About nine times more money for the bank,
just because of the card in your

150
00:06:40,366 --> 00:06:41,366
wallet.

151
00:06:41,566 --> 00:06:45,166
So why would a bank want you to
carry the expensive card?

152
00:06:45,166 --> 00:06:47,886
Because that fee is what
pays for the perks.

153
00:06:47,886 --> 00:06:48,886
The points.

154
00:06:48,766 --> 00:06:49,766
The cash back.

155
00:06:49,822 --> 00:06:50,822
The travel miles.

156
00:06:50,782 --> 00:06:52,062
The airport lounges.

157
00:06:52,062 --> 00:06:56,142
Your bank collects more from the store,
and hands some of it back to you

158
00:06:56,142 --> 00:06:57,502
as rewards.

159
00:06:57,502 --> 00:07:00,495
The more you spend, the more it collects.

160
00:07:00,654 --> 00:07:04,894
So banks compete hard for the
people who spend the most.

161
00:07:04,895 --> 00:07:08,734
That's why the cards with the best
rewards are usually the ones that cost

162
00:07:08,734 --> 00:07:09,854
stores the most.

163
00:07:09,854 --> 00:07:11,600
And the store can't see that coming.

164
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,120
The card is in your pocket until
the very last second.

165
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:16,400
Quick thing.

166
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:19,600
If you like videos that follow
the money, subscribe.

167
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:20,800
It costs nothing.

168
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,464
And unlike your rewards points...

169
00:07:22,623 --> 00:07:25,423
nobody else is paying for it.

170
00:07:26,399 --> 00:07:29,599
Part 4 The People Who Pay Without Swiping

171
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:31,359
So the store pays the fee.

172
00:07:31,359 --> 00:07:32,799
But who pays the store?

173
00:07:32,799 --> 00:07:34,654
Stores have a few options.

174
00:07:34,814 --> 00:07:35,814
In most U.S.

175
00:07:35,694 --> 00:07:38,654
states, they're allowed to add a
surcharge for credit cards.

176
00:07:38,654 --> 00:07:41,774
Under Visa's rules, it's
capped at three percent.

177
00:07:41,774 --> 00:07:44,894
Some gas stations do it, and
give you a cash price.

178
00:07:44,894 --> 00:07:46,495
But most stores don't.

179
00:07:46,655 --> 00:07:48,575
Surcharges annoy customers.

180
00:07:48,575 --> 00:07:51,935
So instead, they raise their
prices a little...

181
00:07:51,935 --> 00:07:53,455
for everyone.

182
00:07:54,118 --> 00:07:57,158
And everyone includes people
paying with cash.

183
00:07:57,158 --> 00:07:59,798
And people paying with a basic debit card.

184
00:07:59,798 --> 00:08:03,819
They pay the same higher price, and
they get no points back.

185
00:08:04,219 --> 00:08:09,099
In 2010, economists at the Federal Reserve
Bank of Boston tried to measure

186
00:08:09,099 --> 00:08:11,019
how big that hidden transfer is.

187
00:08:11,019 --> 00:08:15,179
They estimated that, on average, each
household paying with cash was paying

188
00:08:15,179 --> 00:08:18,459
one hundred and forty-nine
dollars a year to card users.

189
00:08:18,459 --> 00:08:21,556
And each card-paying household was
receiving one thousand, one hundred and

190
00:08:21,556 --> 00:08:22,556
thirty-three dollars

191
00:08:23,099 --> 00:08:24,799
a year from cash users.

192
00:08:25,119 --> 00:08:29,359
They also estimated that about
seventy-nine percent of banks' revenue from

193
00:08:29,359 --> 00:08:33,425
credit card merchant fees came,
indirectly, from cash payers.

194
00:08:33,585 --> 00:08:36,865
And the money flowed from poorer
households to richer ones.

195
00:08:36,865 --> 00:08:40,225
The lowest-income households paid
about twenty-one dollars a year.

196
00:08:40,225 --> 00:08:44,230
The highest-income households received
about seven hundred and fifty.

197
00:08:44,550 --> 00:08:49,590
That study is from 2010, and some payments
experts dispute its assumptions.

198
00:08:49,590 --> 00:08:51,950
But newer research points the same way.

199
00:08:51,950 --> 00:08:56,670
A 2023 study published by the Federal
Reserve estimated that rewards cards

200
00:08:56,670 --> 00:08:59,710
redistribute about fifteen
billion dollars a year.

201
00:08:59,710 --> 00:09:02,750
From less educated people to
more educated ones.

202
00:09:02,750 --> 00:09:06,030
And from poorer areas to richer ones.

203
00:09:06,350 --> 00:09:08,110
There's a second split, too.

204
00:09:08,110 --> 00:09:11,310
Inside the group of people who
do use credit cards.

205
00:09:11,310 --> 00:09:14,990
The people who gain the most are the ones
who pay their bill in full every

206
00:09:14,990 --> 00:09:15,990
month.

207
00:09:15,822 --> 00:09:18,702
They collect the points, and never
pay a cent of interest.

208
00:09:18,702 --> 00:09:22,302
The ones who carry a balance end up paying
more in interest than they ever

209
00:09:22,302 --> 00:09:24,222
get back in rewards.

210
00:09:24,610 --> 00:09:27,570
To be fair, cards aren't just a cost.

211
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:29,890
They protect you from fraud.

212
00:09:29,890 --> 00:09:33,352
They let stores skip the risk of
handling piles of cash.

213
00:09:33,512 --> 00:09:38,072
And the industry argues that cards help
stores sell more, which pays for

214
00:09:38,072 --> 00:09:39,752
part of the fee.

215
00:09:39,752 --> 00:09:41,272
But the points?

216
00:09:41,272 --> 00:09:43,272
Those were never free.

217
00:09:44,856 --> 00:09:46,936
Part 5 The Twenty-Year Fight

218
00:09:46,936 --> 00:09:50,616
Stores have been fighting these
fees for a long time.

219
00:09:50,616 --> 00:09:56,446
In 2005, merchants filed a giant class
action against Visa, Mastercard and

220
00:09:56,446 --> 00:09:57,446
the big banks.

221
00:09:57,326 --> 00:10:01,166
More than twenty years later, it
still isn't fully over.

222
00:10:01,166 --> 00:10:06,126
In 2012, the two sides agreed on a
settlement worth about seven billion

223
00:10:06,126 --> 00:10:07,126
dollars.

224
00:10:07,281 --> 00:10:10,161
An appeals court threw it out in 2016.

225
00:10:10,161 --> 00:10:14,001
A second deal, for at least five and a
half billion dollars, was approved

226
00:10:14,001 --> 00:10:15,361
in 2019.

227
00:10:15,361 --> 00:10:16,662
That covered the money.

228
00:10:16,902 --> 00:10:19,462
The fight over the rules kept going.

229
00:10:19,462 --> 00:10:24,182
Meanwhile, in 2010, Congress stepped
in, but only for debit cards.

230
00:10:24,182 --> 00:10:28,379
The Durbin Amendment led to the cap we
saw earlier, twenty-one cents plus

231
00:10:28,379 --> 00:10:30,219
a little, for big banks.

232
00:10:30,219 --> 00:10:32,619
Credit cards were left alone.

233
00:10:32,939 --> 00:10:35,478
And here's where the money
found a side door.

234
00:10:35,638 --> 00:10:39,878
The cap only applies to banks with more
than ten billion dollars in assets.

235
00:10:39,878 --> 00:10:43,078
Smaller banks can still charge
the old, higher rates.

236
00:10:43,078 --> 00:10:46,569
So a whole new kind of company
grew up around that gap.

237
00:10:46,809 --> 00:10:49,640
App-based banks that partner
with small banks.

238
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:55,560
Chime, for example, said in its 2025 IPO
filing that debit card interchange

239
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:58,120
made up about half of its revenue.

240
00:10:58,823 --> 00:11:00,823
In 2024, the U.S.

241
00:11:00,823 --> 00:11:05,463
Justice Department sued Visa, accusing it
of illegally monopolizing debit

242
00:11:05,463 --> 00:11:06,463
payments.

243
00:11:06,279 --> 00:11:08,999
The government said more than
sixty percent of U.S.

244
00:11:08,999 --> 00:11:13,159
debit transactions run on Visa's network,
and that Visa collects more than

245
00:11:13,159 --> 00:11:16,199
seven billion dollars a year
in fees from them.

246
00:11:16,199 --> 00:11:19,039
Visa denies it, and is fighting the case.

247
00:11:19,039 --> 00:11:23,599
But in 2025, a judge
refused to throw it out.

248
00:11:23,599 --> 00:11:28,014
Back in the big merchant case, a judge
rejected a new settlement in 2024,

249
00:11:28,254 --> 00:11:30,014
saying it didn't go far enough.

250
00:11:30,014 --> 00:11:31,374
So they tried again.

251
00:11:31,374 --> 00:11:36,489
In June 2026, a federal judge gave
preliminary approval to another deal.

252
00:11:36,729 --> 00:11:40,809
It would cut credit card interchange rates
by a tenth of a percentage point,

253
00:11:40,809 --> 00:11:42,009
for five years.

254
00:11:42,009 --> 00:11:45,849
It would cap the rate on standard credit
cards at one point two five percent,

255
00:11:45,849 --> 00:11:46,867
for eight years.

256
00:11:47,027 --> 00:11:50,547
And it would let stores refuse
some premium cards.

257
00:11:50,707 --> 00:11:52,519
A tenth of a percentage point.

258
00:11:52,679 --> 00:11:55,319
On our hundred dollars, that's ten cents.

259
00:11:55,319 --> 00:11:59,480
The premium card goes from two dollars
forty to two dollars thirty.

260
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:02,933
Some of the biggest retailers
objected that it's far too little.

261
00:12:02,933 --> 00:12:07,492
As this video was made, the deal
still needed final approval.

262
00:12:07,492 --> 00:12:08,492
Part 6 So, Who Pays for Your Points?

263
00:12:07,503 --> 00:12:08,503
For a very different answer,
look across the Atlantic.

264
00:12:07,514 --> 00:12:08,514
In 2015, the European Union
capped interchange fees.

265
00:12:07,525 --> 00:12:08,525
Point three percent on
consumer credit cards.

266
00:12:07,534 --> 00:12:08,534
Point two percent on debit cards.

267
00:12:07,542 --> 00:12:08,542
On a hundred-euro purchase, that's
thirty cents for the bank.

268
00:12:07,555 --> 00:12:08,555
Not two dollars and forty.

269
00:12:07,562 --> 00:12:08,562
A study commissioned by the European
Commission found that interchange on

270
00:12:07,576 --> 00:12:08,576
consumer cards dropped by about
thirty-five percent.

271
00:12:07,586 --> 00:12:08,586
More than two billion euros a year
that stayed with stores.

272
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:08,600
And with fees capped that low, banks have
far less money to hand back as

273
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:08,620
points.

274
00:12:07,621 --> 00:12:08,621
In the UK, some banks reportedly dropped
their rewards cards before the

275
00:12:07,637 --> 00:12:08,637
cap even took effect.

276
00:12:07,642 --> 00:12:08,642
So Europe made a trade.

277
00:12:07,649 --> 00:12:08,649
Cheaper fees for stores.

278
00:12:07,654 --> 00:12:08,654
Fewer perks for card users.

279
00:12:07,661 --> 00:12:08,661
America made the opposite one.

280
00:12:07,668 --> 00:12:08,668
So, when you tap your card,
who really gets paid?

281
00:12:07,681 --> 00:12:08,681
Visa takes a toll.

282
00:12:07,686 --> 00:12:08,686
A few cents, on trillions of dollars.

283
00:12:07,695 --> 00:12:08,695
And it pays some of that back to the
banks, to keep its name on the card.

284
00:12:07,718 --> 00:12:08,718
The store's payment company takes its cut.

285
00:12:07,727 --> 00:12:08,727
Your bank takes the biggest slice, and hands
some of it back to you as points.

286
00:12:07,748 --> 00:12:08,748
And the bill is split between the
store, its customers...

287
00:12:07,761 --> 00:12:08,761
and people who never tapped a card at all.

288
00:12:07,773 --> 00:12:08,773
Your points aren't free.

289
00:12:07,778 --> 00:12:08,778
Everyone at the checkout chips in.

290
00:12:07,786 --> 00:12:08,786
Next time, we follow the money behind one
of the oldest banks in the world.

291
00:12:07,806 --> 00:12:08,806
Credit Suisse survived for one
hundred and sixty-seven years.

292
00:12:07,818 --> 00:12:08,818
Then it died in a single weekend.

293
